(a) Enforcement shall begin with a written notice or violation provided to the owner and occupant. The notice may be delivered personally or posted in a conspicuous location or sent by certified mail. The notice shall contain a deadline of three days for compliance.
(b) If the violation continues past the deadline, the city shall institute legal proceedings charging the person or persons, firm, corporation or agent with a violation of this article. In all cases in which the city institutes a complaint pursuant to this article, a copy of the complaint and summons shall be conspicuously posted at the subject property within three business days of filing the complaint and at least ten days prior to the date of the hearing. Furthermore, a copy of the complaint and summons shall be served in the following manner:
(1) If each owner and party in interest is a resident of the county, service shall be either personal or by statutory overnight delivery, return receipt requested, shall be perfected at least 14 days prior to the date of the hearing, and a return of service, filed with the clerk of court, shall be deemed sufficient proof that service was perfected;
(2) If any owner or party in interest is a resident of this state but resided outside of the county, service shall be perfected by certified mail or statutory overnight delivery, return receipt requested, to the most recent address shown in county tax filings and mailed at least fourteen (14) days prior to the date of the hearing;
(3) Nonresidents of this state, whose mailing address is known, shall be served by certified mail or statutory overnight delivery, return receipt requested, mailed at least 14 days prior to the date of the hearing. For nonresidents whose mailing address is unknown, a notice stating the date, time, and place of the hearing shall be published in the newspaper in which the sheriff's advertisements appear in such county once a week for two consecutive weeks prior to the hearing.
(Ord. No. 2008-08, § 1, 11-10-2008; Ord. No. 2024-06042, § c), 6-4-2024)

277:
(a) The chief of police shall be authorized to suspend or revoke a license previously granted herein. In the event the chief of police seeks to suspend or to revoke a license, he shall give written notification to the licensee of such action, and such notice shall contain a specification of the violation and shall be served upon the licensee at least five days prior to the notice of hearing. The licensee shall be given written notice of the time and place of the hearing.
(b) The chief of police shall be authorized to suspend or revoke a license in the event of any one or more of the following:
(1) The licensee gave false or misleading information in the original application process;
(2) The licensee has knowingly allowed possession, use or sale of alcohol or controlled substances on the premises and/or knowingly allowed possession, use or sale of alcohol or controlled substances to a minor on the premises;
(3) The licensee has knowingly allowed the violation of an ordinance of the city or a violation of any criminal law of the state to occur on the premises;
(4) The licensee has violated any provisions of this article;
(5) The licensee has been convicted of any drug-related, alcohol-related or sex-related crime or crime of moral turpitude in the state or the city regarding an offense which was committed on the premises or which would otherwise violate the provisions of this article; or
(6) The licensee fails to pay any fee, license fee, fine or other amount of money due to the city under this article or any other licensing ordinance of the city.
(c) In the event the chief of police suspends or revokes any license hereunder, the suspension or revocation shall be for a period of not less than one day or more than 365 days, within the discretion of the chief of police; provided, however, that the licensee shall be authorized to continue business operations until the date of the hearing scheduled in accordance with subsection (d) of this section. No licensee or other applicant may apply for a license during any period of suspension or revocation. In any hearing conducted by the chief of police, the chief shall consider, among other things, the severity of the allegations, the evidence submitted and the testimony presented in making any decision on suspension or revocation and the duration of either.
(d) In the event of a suspension or revocation by the chief of police, the licensee may appeal the decision of the chief of police to the mayor and city council by filing a written notice of appeal within ten days from the date of the decision of the chief of police. Thereafter, a hearing shall be scheduled before the mayor and council within 45 days after the date of the notice of appeal by the applicant. After hearing by the mayor and city council, city council may take such action as it deems appropriate, including the upholding of the action of the chief of police or the imposition of such action as the mayor and city council may deem appropriate under the facts. The decision of mayor and city council shall be final. Appeals of the decision of the mayor and city council shall be to the county superior court and shall be filed within 30 days of the final action of mayor and city council. In the event the licensee does not file an appeal of any decision of the chief of police, as provided herein, the decision of the chief of police shall be final.
(Ord. of ?, § XXIX)

292:
(a) Any person desiring to obtain a permit to operate a body art studio shall make application to the city manager or his designated representative.
(b) Applications for a permit shall be made in writing on a form prescribed by the city clerk, wherein the applicant shall agree to conform to all rules and regulations now in effect or as subsequently enacted.
(c) The minimum age for each applicant shall be at least 18 years of age.
(d) An application for a permit shall be accompanied by the following:
(1) A fee, in the amount of $250.00;
(2) The address or location where the body art studio will operate;
(3) A floor sketch of the studio showing windows, doors, room measurements, chairs, tables, and equipment placement for customers and/or staff;
(4) A list of full names, addresses, telephone numbers, and dates of birth of all owner(s), operators(s), and staff (including the owner(s) who will be working in the studio), specifically listing the names of each individual who will perform tattooing, scarification, or body piercing;
(5) Written proof that the applicant is at least 18 years of age;
(6) A written statement of policies and procedures, outlining all operations of the body art studio, including, but not limited to, personnel requirements, customer records, procedures, and sanitation;
(7) Written proof that the studio complies with all applicable provisions of this Code of Ordinances, including, but not limited to, the applicable building, gas, electrical, and plumbing codes and zoning ordinances; and
(8) Criminal background authorization forms for all staff members who will be performing tattooing, scarification, or body piercing.
(e) As a condition precedent to the issuance of a permit, the applicant shall obtain a certificate of inspection from the Habersham Board of Health indicating the studio has been inspected and is in compliance with the terms of this article as well as the requirements of the Department of Health Resources, to the extent the Habersham Board of Health has adopted regulations concerning body art studios.
(Ord. No. 2011-03, § 1, 10-6-2011)

293:
(a) The city shall have 45 days to investigate the application and the criminal background of the applicant. Upon completion of the investigation, the city manager will grant the permit if he finds that:
(1) The required fee has been paid;
(2) The application conforms in all respects to the provisions of this article;
(3) The applicant has not knowingly made a material misrepresentation in the application;
(4) The building, structure, equipment, or location of such studio, as proposed by the applicant, would comply with all zoning and distance laws and would qualify to receive a certificate of occupancy;
(5) The applicant is at least 18 years of age;
(6) The premises covered by the permit are not in violation of any applicable provision of this Code, or other state or federal law relating to health issues, sanitary standards, operation of businesses, tax delinquency, illegal substance sale or use, or crimes of moral turpitude;
(7) The applicant and the staff of the body art studio who will be performing tattooing, scarification, or body piercing do not have a criminal conviction directly related to the occupation for which the permit is sought; and
(8) The applicant has received a permit from the Habersham County Board of Health pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 31-40-2, if applicable.
(b) If the applicant fails to meet the requirements of this chapter, the city manager shall deny the application in writing.
(c) No permit shall be issued pursuant to any provision of this article to any applicant if within 12 months immediately preceding the filing of any application under this article, the same applicant requesting a permit has been denied a permit based in whole or in part upon having furnished fraudulent or untruthful information in any application or having omitted any material fact in any application.
(Ord. No. 2011-03, § 1, 10-6-2011)

300:
(a) Records for each customer shall be kept and maintained by the owner or operator of each studio that describes the procedures administered.
(b) Each customer record shall be created prior to any procedure being performed and shall include:
